  "query_preview": "..The large-scale pattern this evening consists of a\nclosed low spinning off the central California coast while Utah\nremains under a southerly flow ahead of it. An upper jet rounding\nthe base of the low and punching through Arizona this evening is\nhelping to spread tropical moisture northward. Across Utah, PWATs\ncurrently range from 0.6-0.9 inches, which is already close to the\n99th percentile of climatology. Increased moisture advection will\nonly push these values higher over the next couple of days.\n\nAs the jet arrives over Utah, increasing upper diffluence may \nlead to some isolated shower activity tonight, mainly over \nnorthwest Utah. As the deeper moisture arrives after 10z, expect \nprecipitation activity to start increasing across southern Utah. \nThis precipitation will become more widespread across southern \nand central Utah through the day Wednesday. Models indicate a \nbrief decrease in precipitation Wednesday night before increasing \nagain areawide for Thursday. Areas of moderate to heavy \nprecipitation is expected Wednesday and Thursday, and will be \naided in part by potentially strong to severe convection as \nsuggested by hi-res models. If instability does not become \ninhibited too much by cloud cover, there will be plenty of shear \n(40-55+ kt of 0-6km shear) and low-level helicity to work with \nacross southern Utah on Wednesday and across a significant portion\nof the forecast area on Thursday for a few of the stronger storms\nto produce strong winds, hail, and even an outside chance of a \ntornado. There is currently a Marginal Risk for Severe Storms \nacross southern Utah on Wednesday and across southern through \nweest-central Utah on Thursday. Additionally, with the heavy rain,\nthere is also a Slight Risk of Excessive Rainfall across southern\nUtah on Wednesday and across southern through east-central Utah \non Thursday. Also on Thursday, a Marginal Risk for Excessive \nRainfall extends through the remainder of Utah. A Flood Watch is \nin effect across southern through east-central Utah Wednesday \nthrough Thursday as a result. Latest NBM continues to indicate \nwidespread 1 to greater than 2.5 inches of QPF across southern \nthrough east-central Utah Wednesday through Thursday, with 75th \npercentile amounts exceeding 3 inches near Brian Head and Boulder \nMountain. \n\nOn Friday, the upper low is expected to lift across northern Utah\nwhile a trailing wave rotating around the low swings across the\ndesert southwest. The track of the low across northern Utah will\nkeep precipitation confined more to the north on Friday, while\ncooler air arrives into the area (H7 temperatures down to around \n-3C Friday through Saturday night). This will bring snow levels as\nlow as 7000-7500 feet, with accumulating snow of around 2-6\ninches forecast above around 9000 feet, mainly across the \nWasatch and Uinta Mountains. \n\nThe trailing wave swinging around Arizona now looks to redevelop\ninto a closed low which will then linger over the area. As such,\nthere is now increasing likelihood for cool and unsettled weather\nto remain in place through the weekend. Thereafter, high pressure\nlooks to build into the area early next week.",
